      "url": "https://forum.bambulab.com/t/h2c-unusable-for-nearly-two-weeks-faults-and-poor-support/262697?page=2#post_25",
      "title": "H2C unusable for nearly two weeks - faults and poor support",
      "content_html": "\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img alt=\"\" class=\"avatar\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forum.bambulab.com/user_avatar/forum.bambulab.com/harley_s_h2s/48/181036_2.png\" width=\"24\"/> Harley_s_H2S:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I have been offered a new printer, however they want me to post the old one back in original packaging first, which I do not have. Or pay for a full new printer, once that arrives, send the old one back in the boxes. which I think both options are unreasonable.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Actually, both options are standard procedures for Bambu, so I’d recommend taking one of them.</p>\n<p>Bambu Lab won’t send out a replacement printer unless you either return the old one first or essentially order a new one, transfer everything into the new packaging, and send the original one back.</p>\n<p>There is also a third option that apparently wasn’t offered to you? They can send you the packaging separately. However, someone here on the forum tried that, and it took over a month for only the packaging to arrive.</p>\n<hr/>\n<p>Unfortunately, that’s one of the downsides of not keeping the original packaging. No criticism at all, but that’s exactly why people on this forum keep repeating: Keep your packaging! There’s even a dedicated tutorial somewhere here on the forum / reddit showing how to fold the packaging so it takes up as little storage space as possible.</p>\n<hr/>\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img alt=\"\" class=\"avatar\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forum.bambulab.com/user_avatar/forum.bambulab.com/harley_s_h2s/48/181036_2.png\" width=\"24\"/> Harley_s_H2S:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>the printer is not even a month old</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You always know better afterwards, but personally, I would have kept the packaging for at least a month, just in case, given the 30-day return policy. However, everything I’ve written so far has probably become irrelevant because:</p>\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img alt=\"\" class=\"avatar\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forum.bambulab.com/user_avatar/forum.bambulab.com/harley_s_h2s/48/181036_2.png\" width=\"24\"/> Harley_s_H2S:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I have documented the process, so have plenty evidence of the faults caused. As I have said earlier, I paid for the replacement parts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you’ve consciously or unconsciously already decided <strong>to go with the repair option</strong>, that unfortunately <strong>closes off some other possibilities</strong>.</p>\n<hr/>\n<p><strong>My personal opinion and recommendation:</strong> If they’re offering you this option, even though you’ve already gone down the repair route, I’d consider buying a replacement printer at your own expense, after confirming everything with Bambu first.</p>\n<p>Make sure to use a payment provider like PayPal so you’re protected in case anything goes wrong. That’s really important!</p>\n<p>Once the new printer arrives, you can send the old one back. If you don’t receive the money, file a dispute through your payment service provider.</p>\n<hr/>\n<p><strong>If the risk is too high for you</strong>: …and you’ve already mentally written off Bambu anyway, you can also file a dispute immediately through your payment service provider.</p>\n<hr/>\n<p><strong>One more thing to keep in mind:</strong> Opening a dispute doesn’t automatically mean you’ll get your money back. It simply means the payment is put on hold retroactively - while a third party reviews the case. Both you and Bambu need to respond and provide the necessary information. Otherwise, the other party may automatically win the dispute.</p>",
      "date_published": "2026-11-16T02:09:36+00:00",
